---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { url, repository } from '../lib/site';
const builds = `${repository}/actions/workflows/ci.yml`;
---
<Layout title="Pinak MCP — Local or your own Cloud" description="Connect your AI application to Pinak. Choose local stdio or host your own Cloud MCP instance with individual tokens and expiring file links.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">AI CONNECTIONS / MODEL CONTEXT PROTOCOL</span><h1>Ask your AI.<br/><span style="color:var(--accent)">Keep the controls.</span></h1><p>Turn a request into a saved download job. Pinak-MCP connects a compatible AI application to the same download engine and durable queue you use in the terminal.</p></section>
    <div class="callout"><strong>You choose where it runs.</strong> Pinak provides the software. Local runs on your computer; Cloud runs on a server you operate. There is no Pinak-hosted MCP or download service.</div>
    <section class="mcp-options" aria-label="Choose your MCP edition">
      <article class="mcp-option" id="local"><span class="eyebrow">01 / ON YOUR DEVICE</span><h2>Local MCP</h2><p>Your AI application launches a local Python process over <strong>stdio</strong>. Media stays in your local output folder. No server, domain, or public endpoint to maintain.</p><ul><li>Python 3.10+ and a client that can launch local MCP processes.</li><li>Fourteen tools for queues, history, metadata, and diagnostics.</li><li>CLI and MCP share your local queue and history.</li><li>Repository guide: <code>MCP(Local)</code>.</li></ul><a class="button" href={url('/docs/mcp-local/')}>Set up Local MCP →</a><p class="small">Installer artifact: <code>pinak-mcp-install</code></p></article>
      <article class="mcp-option" id="cloud"><span class="eyebrow">02 / ON YOUR OWN SERVER</span><h2>Cloud MCP</h2><p>Host an HTTPS <strong>Streamable HTTP</strong> endpoint for a small group of trusted users. Each user gets an individual bearer token, separate queue, database, download folder, and process.</p><ul><li>Your own VPS or Render account, storage, bandwidth, and credentials.</li><li>Fifteen tools, including expiring <code>file_link</code> downloads.</li><li>Media is saved on your server; a link retrieves it to your device.</li><li>Repository guide: <code>MCP(cloud)</code>.</li></ul><a class="button button-ghost" href={url('/docs/mcp-cloud/')}>Host your Cloud MCP →</a><p class="small">Installer artifact: <code>pinak-mcp-cloud-install</code></p></article>
    </section>
    <article class="content-narrow" style="margin-left:0">
      <h2>From a request to a saved file</h2><ol class="mcp-workflow"><li><strong>Add.</strong> Ask your AI to queue a supported public media URL you have permission to save. Options are saved with the job.</li><li><strong>Start.</strong> Ask it to start the queue. Adding a job alone does not download anything.</li><li><strong>Inspect.</strong> Check progress, saved options, errors, and files. Retry failed jobs or remove completed records while keeping downloaded files.</li><li><strong>Open.</strong> Local files stay on your device. In Cloud, request a file link after completion; it expires after five minutes by default.</li></ol>
      <div class="example-terminal mcp-example"><div class="terminal-top">EXAMPLE REQUEST / NOT A LIVE AI CONNECTION</div><div class="terminal-body">“Queue this media URL, start the queue, and show me its download status.”</div></div>
      <h2>Get the right installer</h2><p>Open a successful <strong>main-branch push</strong> run in <a href={builds}>GitHub CI builds ↗</a>, then download the edition’s artifact. GitHub sign-in is required; artifacts expire after 90 days. Extract the bundle, verify <code>SHA256SUMS</code>, and follow its <code>INSTALL.md</code>.</p><p>Bundles contain the application wheel and setup files. They exclude the website, media showcase, Git history, and planning folders. Installation still fetches dependencies. These are source snapshots; do not assume the PyPI release has the same MCP features.</p>
      <h2>Choose a compatible AI application</h2><p>Local needs a client that can launch stdio processes. Cloud needs Streamable HTTP with a custom <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code> header. OAuth-only or legacy SSE-only remote clients are not supported by this Cloud setup. Configuration formats differ between applications.</p><p>Your AI provider may receive prompts and returned metadata. Local files remain local; Cloud files remain on your server until retrieved. Expiring file links authorize anyone holding the link until expiry.</p>
      <h2>What has been verified</h2><p>Local SDK interoperability and Windows Cursor checks passed. Cloud has passed local Docker Compose, verified HTTPS, real downloads, user isolation, and restart-persistence checks. Live VPS/Render account deployments remain unverified; the recipes are supplied for operators to validate in their own environments.</p><p>Cloud is designed for trusted users on one service instance. Storage limits are soft; process separation is not an operating-system sandbox. <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/MCP%28cloud%29/SECURITY.md`}>Read Cloud security boundaries ↗</a>.</p>
      <div class="guide-links"><a class="button button-ghost" href={url('/docs/mcp/')}>Read the MCP guide →</a><a class="text-link" href={`${repository}/blob/main/MCP%28cloud%29/HOSTING_STEPS.md`}>Step-by-step hosting ↗</a></div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
